/// \defgroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b Update modules and their factories, part B (DelayedLuaEventUpdate to HeroModeSpecialAbilityUpdate)
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Seventeen of BFME 2's update modules, from DelayedLuaEventUpdate
///        to HeroModeSpecialAbilityUpdate in alphabetical order, seen
///        through the factory functions that create them: eleven module
///        classes with their instance factories and thirteen module data
///        classes with their data factories.
///
/// Each module here is attached to an object by a `Behavior = <ModuleName>
/// ModuleTag_...` line inside an `Object` block in INI. This group covers
/// how a module and its data come into existence and which INI fields the
/// data accepts, read from the retail field tables. It does not describe
/// what the modules do every logic frame; where a class page says what a
/// module is for, it labels the source (Zero Hour, BFME 1, or the field
/// names themselves). For several modules only one of the two factories is
/// documented here; the class pages name the other one.
///
/// \par Two factories per module name
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance factory,
/// a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the registrations
/// are listed in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. All seventeen
/// names are spelled exactly like their classes and are registered as
/// logic modules (module type 0).
/// - The **data factory** runs while INI loads.
///   ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it with the INI reader for
///   each module line. It creates the module data with its constructor
///   defaults and, when the reader is not null, parses the module's block
///   into it with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the data class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse.
/// - The **instance factory** runs when an object is built.
///   ModuleFactory::newModule calls it with the new object and the module
///   data parsed earlier, and it creates the module.
///
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories walks through this flow
/// step by step; it applies here unchanged. Neither factory is called
/// directly: the module factory reaches them only through the registered
/// pointers, so no direct caller is known.
///
/// \par Interface masks
/// Sixteen of the seventeen registrations carry the update interface bit
/// alone. EmotionTrackerUpdate's adds the bit Zero Hour uses for the
/// destroy interface (\c MODULEINTERFACE_DESTROY in Zero Hour's
/// `Common/Module.h`); this page does not confirm what that bit means in
/// BFME 2.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - One module data object exists per module line per object template. It
///   is shared, read-only in practice, by every object built from that
///   template, and the module factory keeps it in its own list.
/// - One module exists per object whose template lists it, owned by that
///   object. Zero Hour: the object's destructor deletes its modules and the
///   module factory's destructor deletes the module data.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Every instance factory here allocates the module with the global
///   \c operator \c new (\ref sub_memory) at the class's size, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data, and returns
///   the module as the Module base the factory stores. An exception frame
///   around the constructor frees the block if the constructor throws, as
///   a C++ new-expression requires; when the allocation returns null the
///   constructor is skipped and null is returned.
/// - Every data factory here has the same frame, runs the data class's
///   default constructor, and then calls INI::initFromINIMultiProc outside
///   the exception frame, so an INI error thrown while parsing does not
///   free the new data.
/// - Zero Hour generates both functions inline from the module macros in
///   `Common/Module.h`; there the module comes from the class's memory pool
///   and the data from a tagged \c new. The BFME 2 bodies call the global
///   \c operator \c new for both.
///
/// \par Base classes
/// Read from the retail constructors the instance factories run:
/// - DelayedLuaEventUpdate, DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate,
///   DestroyEnvironmentUpdate, DetachableRiderUpdate, EmotionTrackerUpdate,
///   EntEnragedUpdate, FadeAndDieOrnamentUpdate and FloodUpdate each start
///   by running UpdateModule's constructor, so they derive directly from
///   UpdateModule (some also implement further interfaces; see their
///   pages).
/// - GiveUpgradeUpdate, GloriousChargeUpdate and
///   HeroModeSpecialAbilityUpdate each start by running the constructor
///   that SpecialAbilityUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance runs for a plain
///   SpecialAbilityUpdate, so they derive from SpecialAbilityUpdate (a
///   structural inference from the retail bodies). The data classes of the
///   first two likewise start by running the constructor that
///   SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData runs; that it is
///   SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData's own constructor is the same kind of
///   structural inference, and its exact identity is not proven.
///   Their \c buildFieldParse calls
///   SpecialAbilityUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse before registering
///   their own table, so their INI blocks accept the shared
///   special-ability fields as well; \ref grp_w1022_ai_special_ability_update_factories
///   describes that shared table.
///
/// \par Origins
/// Zero Hour has DemoTrapUpdate, DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdate,
/// FireSpreadUpdate, FireWeaponUpdate, FloatUpdate and HeightDieUpdate.
/// Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree has sources for all the other modules here
/// except FloodUpdate, so those already exist in BFME 1. Original Generals,
/// whose source ships alongside Zero Hour's in the EA reference, registers
/// the same six as Zero Hour plus DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate, which
/// Zero Hour dropped, and none of the others. FloodUpdate appears in none
/// of these references. Of the Zero Hour modules, five accept
/// exactly the INI field names of their Zero Hour counterparts;
/// FireWeaponUpdate's table differs completely (see its data class). All
/// INI field names on these pages are read from the retail executable's
/// own field tables.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their module tags and
///   every field listed on the data class pages. Several fields name other
///   definitions by text: FX lists (\c parseFXList), object creation lists
///   (\c parseObjectCreationList) and weapons (\c parseWeaponTemplate).
///   This page does not establish whether a misspelled name is reported
///   when the INI loads.
/// - \c GridDecalTemplate (DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData) is
///   not a reference: it is written inline as its own sub-block, which
///   RadiusDecalTemplate::parseRadiusDecalTemplate parses with a sub-table
///   of its own (listed on that data class's page).
/// - Hard-coded: the seventeen module names, their base classes and what
///   each class does with its fields. A new kind of update needs a new
///   class and a new ModuleFactory::init registration
///   (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Modules registered here without a data factory documented on this
///   page still have one; its fields are not listed here.
/// - Multiplayer: all seventeen are logic modules and their data comes from
///   `Object` blocks, so every machine must load identical values
///   (\ref sub_rts_model_modding, \ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: an object saves each module's state as a block keyed by its
///   module tag (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); renaming a tag in a
///   mod orphans that state in old saves.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Every field these pages call a duration (for example \c StartTime,
/// \c SpeechDuration and \c InitialDelay) is parsed by
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which turns milliseconds into whole logic
/// frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details), so a
/// value written in INI keeps its meaning in seconds, up to rounding, if
/// the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) changes. Constructor defaults do
/// not go through that conversion:
/// - A default the constructor stores as a literal is a fixed frame count,
///   so a change to the logic rate changes what it means in seconds. Among
///   the duration defaults these pages give, the only nonzero literal is
///   DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData's \c DestructionTime of 100; the
///   others, apart from \c ScanDelayTime below, are 0, which means the
///   same at any rate.
/// - EntEnragedUpdateModuleData's \c ScanDelayTime default is not a
///   literal: the constructor copies the engine's logic frames-per-second
///   value, so the default is one second at the rate in force when the
///   data is constructed.
///
/// \see \ref sub_objects_modules, \ref sub_rts_model_flow,
///      \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories


/// \class DelayedLuaEventUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Update module registered as "DelayedLuaEventUpdate"; it holds a
///        list of delayed Lua events and starts asleep.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, constructs a
/// DelayedLuaEventList member, clears its own state and puts the module to
/// sleep indefinitely with UpdateModule::setWakeFrame, so it does no work
/// until something wakes it. The name and the list member suggest a
/// module that holds Lua script events to be fired later; what queues them
/// and when they fire is not described here. Its data factory,
/// DelayedLuaEventUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, is not documented
/// on this page. BFME 1 has the class (its sources sit with the script
/// engine in Open-BFME-1's \c game/ tree); Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see DelayedLuaEventUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, \ref sub_scripting

/// \fn Module *DelayedLuaEventUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DelayedLuaEventUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by
///                    DelayedLuaEventUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DelayedLuaEventUpdate


/// \class DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Update module registered as "DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate".
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor and then only installs
/// the class's vtables, including one for a further interface the class
/// implements besides the update module bases. The name suggests a
/// weapon set upgrade applied after a delay; this page does not confirm
/// that. Its data factory,
/// DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, is not
/// documented here. BFME 1 has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// Generals (not Zero Hour): DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate derives from
/// UpdateModule and DelayedUpgradeUpdateInterface, an interface whose
/// \c setDelay is commented as starting the upgrade's countdown and whose
/// \c isTriggeredBy asks whether an upgrade mask would trigger it.
/// Generals' own implementation of both is empty (\c setDelay ignores its
/// argument, \c isTriggeredBy returns false) and its data has no fields of
/// its own. The BFME 2 constructor's extra vtable fits that shape: one
/// further interface beside the update module bases (a structural
/// inference; what BFME 2's implementation does is not described here).
///
/// \see DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line,
///                    built by
///                    DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DelayedWeaponSetUpgradeUpdate


/// \class DemoTrapUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for the "DemoTrapUpdate" module: proximity mode,
///        weapon slots and detonation settings.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// DemoTrapUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template. The module itself is created by
/// DemoTrapUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, which is not documented here.
/// Zero Hour: DemoTrapUpdate handles a demolition trap's proximity
/// triggering. BFME 2 keeps the Zero Hour field names unchanged.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - booleans: \c DefaultProximityMode,
///   \c AutoDetonationWithFriendsInvolved and \c DetonateWhenKilled;
/// - weapon slots chosen by name from a lookup list:
///   \c DetonationWeaponSlot, \c ProximityModeWeaponSlot and
///   \c ManualModeWeaponSlot;
/// - \c TriggerDetonationRange, a real number;
/// - \c IgnoreTargetTypes, read by a dedicated parser (Zero Hour: a KindOf
///   mask, that is, a set of object kinds);
/// - \c ScanRate, a duration;
/// - \c DetonationWeapon, a weapon looked up by name.
///
/// The constructor sets every one of these to zero, false or none.
///
/// \see DemoTrapUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DemoTrapUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DemoTrapUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// while an `Object` block is read. It constructs DemoTrapUpdateModuleData
/// with its defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and DemoTrapUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see DemoTrapUpdateModuleData


/// \class DestroyEnvironmentUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Update module registered as "DestroyEnvironmentUpdate": after a
///        start delay it acts on one adopted object for a set time, then
///        releases it.
///
/// Besides its UpdateModule part it keeps two values: the logic frame its
/// timing is measured from (the start frame) and the ID of the object it
/// acts on. Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, sets the start
/// frame to the "sleep forever" value and the object ID to none (0); when
/// it is given module data, it then sets the start frame to the current
/// logic frame from TheGameLogic.
///
/// DestroyEnvironmentUpdate::update, at contract level:
/// - Without module data it sleeps forever.
/// - When it has no object yet, it searches the game's objects for one
///   that matches and keeps that object's ID; when none matches it sleeps
///   forever. The match criteria are not described here.
/// - Until \c StartTime frames have passed since the start frame, it
///   sleeps for the frames that remain.
/// - For the next \c DestructionTime frames it acts on the adopted object
///   every frame. After that it releases the object, which can destroy it
///   through GameLogic::destroyObject, and forgets the ID.
///
/// The destructor releases the object in the same way, so removing the
/// module also releases its object. DestroyEnvironmentUpdate::xfer saves
/// the start frame and the object ID after the UpdateModule part. BFME 1
/// has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see DestroyEnvironmentUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *DestroyEnvironmentUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DestroyEnvironmentUpdate":
///        creates the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData for
///                    this module line, shared with every other object of
///                    the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DestroyEnvironmentUpdate,
///      DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for DestroyEnvironmentUpdate: a start time and a
///        destruction time.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table: \c StartTime and
/// \c DestructionTime, both durations: the module waits \c StartTime from
/// its start frame, then acts on its object for \c DestructionTime (see
/// DestroyEnvironmentUpdate). The constructor sets \c StartTime to
/// 0 and \c DestructionTime to 100. The default is stored without the
/// millisecond conversion INI values get, so it is a count of logic frames
/// (see the group page).
///
/// \see DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DestroyEnvironmentUpdate": builds
///        the module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData with its defaults,
/// then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see DestroyEnvironmentUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DestroyEnvironmentUpdateModuleData


/// \class DetachableRiderUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Update module registered as "DetachableRiderUpdate", whose name
///        and fields suggest units whose rider can be separated from the
///        mount.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, clears two flags and
/// puts the module to sleep indefinitely with UpdateModule::setWakeFrame,
/// so it does no work until something wakes it. The data fields
/// (DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData) are named for the rider's
/// sub-objects, a weapon slot for when the unit is riderless, a death
/// entry, whether a riderless horde flees (\c RiderlessHordeFlees) and
/// whether a riderless unit is removed from its horde
/// (\c RemoveRiderlessFromHorde). How the module uses them, and what
/// triggers a detachment, is not described here. BFME 1 has the class;
/// Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see DetachableRiderUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *DetachableRiderUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "DetachableRiderUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData for
///                    this module line, shared with every other object of
///                    the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see DetachableRiderUpdate,
///      DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for DetachableRiderUpdate: rider sub-objects, the
///        riderless weapon slot and horde behaviour.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - \c RiderSubObjects, a list of names (model sub-object names, going by
///   the field name); INI::parseAsciiStringVector clears the list and then
///   reads every name on the line, so a later line replaces an earlier one;
/// - \c RiderlessWeaponSlot, a weapon slot chosen by name from the same
///   lookup list DemoTrapUpdateModuleData's slot fields use;
/// - \c RiderlessHordeFlees and \c RemoveRiderlessFromHorde, booleans;
/// - \c DeathEntry, read by a dedicated parser this page does not
///   describe.
///
/// \see DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DetachableRiderUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then,
/// when \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc
/// and DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see DetachableRiderUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      DetachableRiderUpdateModuleData


/// \class DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for the "DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdate" module:
///        how an object's shroud-clearing range grows and shrinks.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData. The
/// module itself is created by
/// DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance, which is not
/// documented here. Zero Hour: the module changes the object's shroud
/// clearing range over time. BFME 2 keeps the Zero Hour field names
/// unchanged.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - durations: \c ChangeInterval, \c GrowInterval, \c ShrinkDelay,
///   \c ShrinkTime, \c GrowDelay and \c GrowTime, all 0 by default;
/// - \c FinalVision, a real number, 0 by default;
/// - \c GridDecalTemplate, a radius decal written inline as its own
///   sub-block rather than a reference to a separate definition.
///   RadiusDecalTemplate::parseRadiusDecalTemplate parses that sub-block
///   with a sub-table of its own. Zero Hour's sub-table has \c Texture,
///   \c Style, \c OpacityMin, \c OpacityMax, \c OpacityThrobTime, \c Color
///   and \c OnlyVisibleToOwningPlayer. BFME 2's, read from the retail
///   table, keeps those names and adds \c Texture2,
///   \c RotationsPerMinute, \c MinRadius, \c MaxRadius,
///   \c MaxSelectedUnits and \c SpiralAcceleration.
///
/// \see DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdate":
///        builds the module data for one module line and parses its INI
///        block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData with its
/// defaults, then, when \p ini is not null, parses the block with
/// INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see DynamicShroudClearingRangeUpdateModuleData


/// \class EmotionTrackerUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Update module registered as "EmotionTrackerUpdate", whose fields
///        cover fear, taunting and quarrels.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor, sets up a further
/// one-slot interface beside the update module bases, and then sets up the
/// module's own state. Its registration is the only one in this group with
/// a second interface bit (see the group page). The data fields
/// (EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData) name what a unit is afraid of, what it
/// taunts or points at, scan distances for heroes and for things to fear,
/// a quarrel probability, a fear immunity level and a list of emotion
/// entries; how the module turns these into behaviour is not described
/// here. BFME 1 has the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see EmotionTrackerUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *EmotionTrackerUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "EmotionTrackerUpdate": creates
///        the module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData for
///                    this module line, shared with every other object of
///                    the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see EmotionTrackerUpdate,
///      EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for EmotionTrackerUpdate: fear, taunt and quarrel
///        settings plus a list of emotions.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - object filters (iniParseObjectFilter): \c TauntAndPointExcluded,
///   \c AfraidOf, \c AlwaysAfraidOf and \c PointAt;
/// - real numbers: \c TauntAndPointDistance, \c HeroScanDistance and
///   \c FearScanDistance;
/// - \c TauntAndPointUpdateDelay, a duration;
/// - \c QuarrelProbability, a percentage stored as a fraction
///   (INI::parsePercentToReal);
/// - \c IgnoreVeterancy, a boolean, and \c ImmuneToFearLevel, an integer;
/// - \c AddEmotion, parsed by
///   EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData::IniParseAddEmotion; going by its
///   name, each such line adds one emotion entry.
///
/// \see EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "EmotionTrackerUpdate": builds the
///        module data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then,
/// when \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc
/// and EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see EmotionTrackerUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      EmotionTrackerUpdateModuleData


/// \class EntEnragedUpdate
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Update module registered as "EntEnragedUpdate"; its fields
///        describe a periodic scan and a timed enraged state.
///
/// Its constructor runs UpdateModule's constructor and then sets up the
/// module's own state. The data fields (EntEnragedUpdateModuleData) are
/// named for a scan delay and a scan distance, two object filters (hated
/// objects and friendly dead), an enraged time, a time until the unit can
/// rage again, a transition time, and FX for the transition and for a buff
/// turning on and off. Whether the scan is what sets off the rage, and
/// what the rage changes on the unit, is not described here. BFME 1 has
/// the class; Zero Hour does not.
///
/// \see EntEnragedUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      EntEnragedUpdateModuleData

/// \fn Module *EntEnragedUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "EntEnragedUpdate": creates the
///        module for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's EntEnragedUpdateModuleData for this
///                    module line, shared with every other object of the
///                    template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see EntEnragedUpdate, EntEnragedUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class EntEnragedUpdateModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1023_update_module_factories_b
/// \brief Module data for EntEnragedUpdate: scan, rage timing, filters and
///        FX.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// EntEnragedUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData, the data factory
/// registered for "EntEnragedUpdate".
///
/// \par Modding
/// Fields, read from the retail table:
/// - durations: \c ScanDelayTime, \c EnragedTime,
///   \c TimeUntilCanRageAgain and \c EnragedTransitionTime. The last three
///   are 0 by default. \c ScanDelayTime defaults to one second's worth of
///   logic frames: the constructor copies the engine's logic
///   frames-per-second value into it (see the group page);
/// - \c ScanDistance, a real number, 300 by default;
/// - object filters (iniParseObjectFilter): \c HatedObjectFilter and
///   \c FriendlyDeadFilter;
/// - FX lists: \c EnragedTransitionFX, \c EnragedOnBuffFX and
///   \c EnragedOffBuffFX, none by default.
///
/// \see EntEnragedUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *EntEnragedUpdateModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "EntEnragedUpdate": builds the module
///        data for one module line and parses its INI block.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// It constructs EntEnragedUpdateModuleData with its defaults, then, when
/// \p ini is not null, parses the block with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and
/// EntEnragedUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse.
///
/// \param ini  The INI reader positioned at the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores,
///         or null when the allocation fails.
/// \see EntEnragedUpdate::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      EntEnragedUpdateModuleData


